      "id": "",
      "title": "Depersonalization/Derealization Disorder - Psychiatry - Merck Manual Professional Edition",
      "url": "",
      "publishedDate": "2025-06-03T00:56:20.000Z",
      "summary": "Summary:\n\n- The Merck Manual defines Depersonalization/Derealization Disorder as a dissociative disorder characterized by persistent or recurrent experiences of being detached from oneself (depersonalization) or from the surrounding environment (derealization). People often feel like an outside observer of their life.\n- It is typically triggered by severe stress and is diagnosed when these experiences are persistent/recurrent and impair functioning, after ruling out other causes.\n- Treatment combines psychotherapy with medications directed at comorbid depression or anxiety.\n- Epidemiology notes that while transient depersonalization/derealization experiences are common (25–75% lifetime), only about 1–2% meet criteria for the disorder.\n- Onset is often in adolescence (mean age ~16), with equal prevalence in men and women; onset after age 25 is uncommon.\n- Etiology frequently involves severe early-life stress or abuse, with episodes triggered by ongoing stress, mood or anxiety disorders, or illicit drug use (notably marijuana, ketamine).\n\nRelevance to your query (Hungarian): The description aligns with the experience of feeling mentally empty or detached during extreme stress, where one may suddenly feel like they disappear or lose their sense of self. This page explains depersonalization/derealization as a diagnosable condition when such experiences are persistent, impair functioning, and are not better explained by other conditions. If the feeling you described is momentary or situational, it may be a transient episode rather than the disorder. Consider consulting a clinician if symptoms are frequent, distressing, or impairing.",
      "image": "",
