      "title": "Depersonalization-derealization disorder - Symptoms and causes - Mayo Clinic",
      "url": "",
      "publishedDate": "2025-09-05T00:00:00.000Z",
      "summary": "Summary:\nThe Mayo Clinic page explains depersonalization-derealization disorder as a condition where you frequently feel detached from yourself (depersonalization) or as if your surroundings aren’t real (derealization), sometimes both. It notes that many people experience brief episodes, but when these feelings are persistent or recurrent and interfere with daily functioning, it may be this disorder. It often follows trauma or extreme stress. Main treatment is psychotherapy, with medications used in some cases. Symptoms include feeling detached from thoughts or body, sensing you’re observing yourself, emotional numbness, and perceptions that surroundings are unreal or distorted. Episodes can last hours to months and may improve or worsen over time. When to seek medical advice: if these feelings are persistent or impair functioning. The page is in English; no Indonesian or Hungarian content is provided. If you’re asking whether extreme stress can cause lasting spiritual or existential emptiness, this page indicates such depersonalization/derealization experiences can become chronic and disrupt life, and professional evaluation is recommended.",
